<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!-- ================================================================
         MODERN COLONIAL INTERFACE — global palette.

         Design goal: dark, sober, elegant. Cool blue-grey colonial
         structure (chrome, panels, borders) paired with a warm aged-
         gold accent for primary actions. Reads as "Age of Empires III
         launcher" without being attached to any single mod's brand.

         Identity colors per mod live in ModAccentBrush only — that
         brush is reserved for decorative spots (mod card border,
         banner fallback, mod initial) and MUST NOT be used for global
         chrome. The rest of the launcher stays neutral so different
         mods look at home in the same shell.

         Naming contract:
           Bg*           = surface backgrounds (darkest → lightest)
           Border*       = outline strokes
           Text*         = body / heading text
           AccentBrush*  = primary action (gold)
           ModAccentBrush* = per-mod identity (decorative only)
           Secondary*    = inactive labels, muted icons
           Success/Error/Warning/Info = semantic state colors
           Mp*           = multiplayer-specific (blue palette)
           Tint*         = low-alpha hover fills
           Fg*           = hover/active foreground tones
         ================================================================ -->

    <!-- =========================================================
         CORE SURFACES — the darkest backdrop progressively lifted
         through panel / panel-alt / neutral layers. Cool-grey-blue
         undertone (vs. the old warm-near-black) so the gold accent
         reads warm by contrast.
         ========================================================= -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BgBase" Color="#0E1113"/>           <!-- main app background -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BgPanel" Color="#15191C"/>          <!-- cards, surfaces inside the main view -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BgPanelAlt" Color="#1B2025"/>       <!-- slightly raised panel variant -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BgNeutral" Color="#2A323A"/>        <!-- neutral controls (dialog buttons idle) -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BgNeutralHover" Color="#36404A"/>   <!-- neutral controls hover -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BgNeutralPressed" Color="#404B57"/> <!-- neutral controls pressed -->

    <!-- =========================================================
         BORDERS — single cool-grey tone used for every outline so
         cards / dialogs / sidebars share the same edge colour. The
         legacy warm "BorderSubtle" (#4e4639) is gone — that tone
         was leftover from the imperial-warm Stitch palette and
         clashed with the cooler Modern Colonial chrome.
         ========================================================= -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BorderSubtle" Color="#3C4650"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BorderSecondary" Color="#3C4650"/>

    <!-- Two-tone edge tokens used by popup menus (gear ContextMenu +
         its submenu, mod-switch popup) to read as a clearly "punched
         out" card over ANY backdrop. The first attempt was a single
         brighter brush at 2px (MpDivider-style lift) — invisible to
         the user. The two-tone approach gives a definitive crispness:
           MenuBorderOuter  = near-black outer rim, visible against
                              bright backgrounds (hero image, light
                              chrome) where a bright rim would wash
                              out.
           MenuBorder       = bright inner line, visible against dark
                              backgrounds (BgPanel / BgBase) where a
                              dark rim would vanish.
         Together (1px outer + 2px inner = 3px effective boundary)
         the menu reads as a discrete card no matter what's behind it
         — the literal "línea negra que lo distinga" the user asked
         for. Same composition recipe is reused in both ActionPanel's
         ContextMenu template and the code-behind mod-switch popup. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MenuBorder" Color="#7C8794"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MenuBorderOuter" Color="#000000"/>

    <!-- =========================================================
         TYPOGRAPHY — warm off-white primary, cool muted secondary.
         The warm primary keeps the launcher from feeling clinical;
         the cool secondary recedes into the chrome so labels don't
         compete with values for attention.
         ========================================================= -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextPrimary" Color="#E8E1D1"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextSecondary" Color="#A8B0B8"/>

    <!-- Disabled text, everywhere. One brush rather than the #666 / #888 / #aaa literals
         that were scattered across eight styles.

         WCAG exempts inactive controls, so this is not about a compliance box — it is that
         #666 measured 2.26:1 on BgNeutral and every one of those styles ALSO put an Opacity
         layer on top of it, landing at 1.57:1. At that point the user cannot read what the
         button they cannot press actually says, and the Opacity was disabling ClearType on
         the caption as a bonus. 4.5:1 on all three surfaces it lands on (BgNeutral 4.51,
         BgPanelAlt 5.69, BgPanel 6.13); it still reads as disabled because it is flat grey
         against near-white enabled text. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextDisabled" Color="#989898"/>

    <!-- =========================================================
         ACCENT — the warm aged-gold "colonial" primary action.
         Used for: PLAY button, Install / Update / Apply CTAs, hover
         underlines on the active nav tab, dorado pill badges, the
         hero title text. NEVER themed per mod — every mod sees the
         same dorado chrome so the launcher identity stays stable
         when the user switches between mods.
         ========================================================= -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccentBrush" Color="#D8B66A"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccentBrushHover" Color="#E3C77B"/>
    <!-- Bottom stop of the PLAY-button gradient — a richer ochre
         that anchors the gradient instead of fading to flat dorado. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccentDeep" Color="#A88A45"/>

    <!-- =========================================================
         PER-MOD ACCENT — decorative only.

         IMPORTANT: this brush IS allowed to vary per active mod
         (UpdateAccentResources publishes a new value on every mod
         switch from profile.AccentColor). It MUST only be consumed
         in places where "this is THIS mod's color" makes sense:
           • Mod card border tint in the Workshop list
           • Hero banner fallback gradient (when the mod has no
             banner image)
           • The big circular "mod initial" badge in the right
             detail panel of the Workshop
           • Selected-row border on the mods list

         It MUST NOT be used in:
           • Global chrome (sidebar / title bar / nav strip)
           • CTAs (PLAY / Install / Update / Apply / Close)
           • Progress bar or status badges
           • Any dialog that's not specifically a "mod detail" view

         Seeded with the legacy WoL red as the fallback so things
         render before the first UpdateAccentResources() runs; once
         the active mod is loaded this gets republished.
         ========================================================= -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ModAccentBrush" Color="#c8102e"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ModAccentBrushHover" Color="#e63950"/>

    <!-- =========================================================
         SECONDARY PALETTE — cool blue-grey muted tones. Used for
         inactive sidebar text, metadata labels, secondary button
         outlines, sub-surfaces. Same cool family as the chrome so
         the dorado accent stays warm-by-contrast.

         Roles mirror the M3 colour-system naming:
           Secondary               muted icons, inactive labels
           SecondaryFixed          high-contrast cool text
           SecondaryContainer      sub-surface tint
           OnSecondary             on top of Secondary
           OnSecondaryContainer    on top of SecondaryContainer
           OnSecondaryFixedVariant muted borders
         ========================================================= -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Secondary" Color="#A8B0B8"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SecondaryFixed" Color="#E8E1D1"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SecondaryContainer" Color="#3C4650"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="OnSecondary" Color="#1A2028"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="OnSecondaryContainer" Color="#A8B0B8"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="OnSecondaryFixedVariant" Color="#3C4650"/>

    <!-- Sidebar / title-bar surface. Brighter than BgPanel so the
         chrome reads as a distinct "navigation" surface and the
         dorado active state still pops against it. Unified between
         the top title bar and any vertical sidebar so the launcher
         chrome feels like one continuous band. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BgSidebar" Color="#314556"/>

    <!-- =========================================================
         MAIN HEADER (MainWindow only) — the two-row chrome from the header
         reference: a near-black title bar over a slightly lifted nav row.

         Deliberately its OWN family rather than a reuse of MpTitleBar (#233648) or
         MpAppBg (#0F1C2E): those two are the multiplayer surface's, shared with
         LobbyWindow and CreateLobbyDialog, and repointing either would restyle
         windows nobody asked about. The header sits above ALL THREE tabs, so it
         answers to the header reference, not to the multiplayer handoff.

         The one value shared with multiplayer is the accent: the reference's
         #2f7ee0 for the active tab's underline and the avatar disc is MpAction
         exactly, so that one is reused rather than duplicated under a new name.
         ========================================================= -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ChromeTitleBg" Color="#0C131B"/>       <!-- title bar -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ChromeNavBg" Color="#141E29"/>         <!-- nav row -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ChromeNavRule" Color="#1E2B38"/>       <!-- nav → content seam -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ChromeRim" Color="#22303E"/>           <!-- capsule, divider -->
    <!-- The version chip's rim alone. ChromeRim (#22303E) is 1.39:1 against the title
         bar — a hairline nobody can see — and the chip is the one place that was
         reported as too faint to read. It gets its own token rather than lifting
         ChromeRim, which also paints the connection capsule and the nav divider: the
         reference specifies #22303e for all three, so brightening the shared brush
         would move two surfaces nobody asked about. 2.09:1 — visible, still quiet. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ChromeVersionRim" Color="#3A4B60"/>
    <!-- Fill of that same chip. It shipped with NO background at all, so it read as
         loose text with a hairline near it rather than as a label, and was reported
         as not standing out. 1.28:1 against the title bar is an elevation, not a
         block: what cuts the chip out is the rim above, which only works once there
         is a surface behind it. Its own key rather than ChromePopupBg, which happens
         to carry the same value but means "menu surface" and paints all three
         dropdowns - one of them changing would silently drag the other along. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ChromeVersionBg" Color="#1C2A3A"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ChromeTextDim" Color="#93A4B5"/>       <!-- wordmark, idle tab -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ChromeTextFaint" Color="#5D7085"/>     <!-- version, IP, ELO -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ChromeTextSoft" Color="#C3CFDA"/>      <!-- "Connected", caption glyphs -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ChromeTextStrong" Color="#E6EDF4"/>    <!-- player name -->
    <!-- One rung above Strong, for the brand button's HOVER only. The hover used to
         land on ChromeTextSoft (11.79:1) and was reported as indistinguishable from
         rest - this is 17.04:1, and stops short of the #FFFFFF that pressed/open
         already use so the three states stay three. MpTextHeading is this exact
         colour, and is deliberately NOT reused: per the family comment above, the
         header does not draw from the multiplayer brushes, which LobbyWindow and
         CreateLobbyDialog share. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ChromeTextBright" Color="#F0F5FB"/>    <!-- brand hover -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ChromeOk" Color="#3FBF6A"/>            <!-- capsule dot -->
    <!-- Surface of every menu that hangs off the header (brand, MODS, notifications).
         One value for the three, because they had three: #1B2025 (a warm grey from the
         old theme), #314556 (literally the colour the title bar itself used to be), and
         #0F1A2B. Measured elevation, not eyeballed — 1.28 against the title bar, 1.16
         against the nav row, 1.30 against the content beneath. It does not need to
         shout: the two-tone rim is what cuts the card out of its backdrop. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ChromePopupBg" Color="#1C2A3A"/>

    <!-- Caption-button states, as KEYS so a single window can restyle its own
         min/max/close without touching the other ~15 that share the TitleBar
         template. These are the DEFAULTS — the gold/red the launcher has always
         had; MainWindow overrides them in its Window.Resources (a DynamicResource
         lookup walks up to the Window before Application, so the override is
         scoped to that window). All six exist because hover is only half of a
         button's state machine: parameterize the background alone and MainWindow
         ends up with a cold fill under a gold glyph that flashes gold when pressed. -->
    <!-- Focus ring of the global TextBox template. A KEY, not a literal, for the same
         reason the caption-button states are: the template trigger targets the inner
         Border BY NAME, so it beats any BorderBrush the call site sets — a field cannot
         opt out of the gold on its own. The multiplayer surface overrides this to blue
         in its own resources; everywhere else keeps the launcher's accent. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextBoxFocusBrush" Color="#D8B66A"/>

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TitleBarButtonHoverBg" Color="#1FD8B66A"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TitleBarButtonHoverFg" Color="#FFF4E0"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TitleBarButtonPressedBg" Color="#33D8B66A"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TitleBarCloseHoverBg" Color="#8E2A22"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TitleBarClosePressedBg" Color="#B85A4A"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TitleBarCloseHoverFg" Color="#FFFFFF"/>

    <!-- =========================================================
         PER-OPERATION PROGRESS GRADIENTS.
         Painted onto the dashboard's progress bar via the
         ProgressBar.Foreground property. Each gradient runs dark →
         mid → bright left-to-right for a premium "metal sheen"
         while still reading as the operation's identity colour.

         Tone mapping:
           Install   blue   → "new content arriving"
           Update    dorado → "refresh, main happy path"
           Repair    amber  → "warning, fixing things"
           Verify    lilac  → "checking, informational"
           Uninstall red    → "destructive, removing"

         All recalibrated to the Modern Colonial palette so the
         progress bar feels like it belongs to the same theme as
         the rest of the launcher.
         ========================================================= -->
    <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="ProgressGradientInstall" StartPoint="0,0" EndPoint="1,0">
        <GradientStop Color="#1F3958" Offset="0.0"/>
        <GradientStop Color="#3F6FAE" Offset="0.5"/>
        <GradientStop Color="#6F9FCE" Offset="1.0"/>
    </LinearGradientBrush>
    <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="ProgressGradientUpdate" StartPoint="0,0" EndPoint="1,0">
        <GradientStop Color="#756036" Offset="0.0"/>
        <GradientStop Color="#A88A45" Offset="0.5"/>
        <GradientStop Color="#D8B66A" Offset="1.0"/>
    </LinearGradientBrush>
    <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="ProgressGradientRepair" StartPoint="0,0" EndPoint="1,0">
        <GradientStop Color="#56401C" Offset="0.0"/>
        <GradientStop Color="#B58A3D" Offset="0.5"/>
        <GradientStop Color="#E0BC6D" Offset="1.0"/>
    </LinearGradientBrush>
    <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="ProgressGradientVerify" StartPoint="0,0" EndPoint="1,0">
        <GradientStop Color="#3D245A" Offset="0.0"/>
        <GradientStop Color="#8055B5" Offset="0.5"/>
        <GradientStop Color="#C49DE0" Offset="1.0"/>
    </LinearGradientBrush>
    <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="ProgressGradientUninstall" StartPoint="0,0" EndPoint="1,0">
        <GradientStop Color="#5A2820" Offset="0.0"/>
        <GradientStop Color="#B85A4A" Offset="0.5"/>
        <GradientStop Color="#D88575" Offset="1.0"/>
    </LinearGradientBrush>

    <!-- Solid tone accents — paint the matching operation icon
         in the progress bar's icon column with the same colour as
         the bar gradient peak, so icon + bar read as one element. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToneInstall" Color="#5F8FBE"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToneUpdate" Color="#D8B66A"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToneRepair" Color="#B58A3D"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToneVerify" Color="#8055B5"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToneUninstall" Color="#B85A4A"/>

    <!-- Misc decorative tones used by older surfaces that still
         reference these keys (PLAY-button gradient internals,
         inactive sidebar Outline tone). -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Outline" Color="#A8B0B8"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DangerAlert" Color="#8E2A22"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DangerAlertFg" Color="#FFB4AB"/>

    <!-- =========================================================
         DESTRUCTIVE — Uninstall, Leave room, error confirmations.
         The new Modern Colonial red is a desaturated terracotta
         (#B85A4A) — reads as "warning, important" without screaming
         like the old pure-blood-red (#8b0000) did. Stays static —
         never themed per mod.
         ========================================================= -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DangerBrush" Color="#B85A4A"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DangerBrushHover" Color="#C66A5A"/>

    <!-- =========================================================
         SEMANTIC STATE COLORS.
         Use these for state-only signals (badges, status pills,
         dot indicators), NOT for primary actions or chrome.
           Success → installed / compatible / connected   (green)
           Error   → uninstall / disconnected / failure   (red)
           Info    → tip / informational                  (blue)
           Warning → repair / pending / outdated          (amber)
         ========================================================= -->
    <!-- Raised from #B85A4A, which measured 4.15:1 on BgBase and is used at 13px for
         validation errors — the one place text most needs to be read. #BE695A keeps the hue
         and reaches 4.83:1 on BgBase / 4.51:1 on BgPanel. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ErrorBrush" Color="#BE695A"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SuccessBrush" Color="#4FA66A"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="InfoBrush" Color="#3F6FAE"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="InfoBrushHover" Color="#4F7FBE"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WarningBrush" Color="#D4A04A"/>

    <!-- =========================================================
         CATALOG (Workshop browser) — selection / CTAs use the
         neutral colonial blue (NOT the per-mod accent — that
         would make the selected row scream in WoL red whenever
         WoL is the active mod). Status badges use desaturated
         tints so the list doesn't feel like a Christmas tree
         when many mods are visible.
         ========================================================= -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CatalogBlue" Color="#3F6FAE"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CatalogBlueHover" Color="#4F7FBE"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CatalogBlueSubtle" Color="#1D2840"/>

    <!-- Status badge fill+stroke pairs. Bg = low-alpha tint of the
         matching Fg colour so badges read as quiet labels rather
         than buttons. All recalibrated for the Modern Colonial
         palette. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StatusInstalledBg" Color="#1D2E22"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StatusInstalledFg" Color="#4FA66A"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StatusNotInstalledBg" Color="#2A323A"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StatusNotInstalledFg" Color="#A8B0B8"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StatusUpdateBg" Color="#2E2818"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StatusUpdateFg" Color="#D8B66A"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StatusIncompatibleBg" Color="#2A203A"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StatusIncompatibleFg" Color="#A98CD6"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StatusErrorBg" Color="#2E1D1A"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StatusErrorFg" Color="#B85A4A"/>

    <!-- =========================================================
         MULTIPLAYER PALETTE.
         The Multiplayer tab uses the same colonial blue family as
         the rest of the launcher's secondary actions. Prefixed Mp*
         so a grep clearly separates multiplayer-specific surfaces
         from shared chrome.
         ========================================================= -->

    <!-- Primary blue CTA + variants. Brighter, more saturated navy-blue for the
         "premium" multiplayer refresh (mockup-driven). Flows into MpPrimary /
         MpOutlineBlue button styles across the whole MP area. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpBlue" Color="#3B82F6"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpBlueHover" Color="#2563EB"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpBluePressed" Color="#1D4ED8"/>
    <!-- Subtle blue tint for selected rows / hover backgrounds /
         outline-variant button fills. Low saturation so it doesn't
         compete with the dark base. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpBlueSubtle" Color="#14264A"/>

    <!-- The amber counterpart of MpBlueSubtle, for a standing notice that is a
         caution rather than an error: paired with the solid WarningBrush as its
         border, exactly like MpBlueSubtle pairs with MpBlue on the countdown
         line. Desaturated to the same degree so it reads as "pay attention"
         without shouting at the player every time they open a room. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpWarnSubtle" Color="#2A2113"/>

    <!-- Multiplayer-specific surfaces — deep navy for the premium refresh so
         the room table / chat cards read as "raised" above the navy tab
         gradient that used to sit behind them. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpSurface" Color="#0F1A2B"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpSurfaceAlt" Color="#182740"/>
    <!-- Card/panel border + hairline divider for all multiplayer
         surfaces. Raised from #2C313A → #3A434F: at the old value it was
         barely lighter than MpSurface (#1B2025), so the lobby's player
         list / info card / chat panel borders washed out against the
         near-black BgBase. This tone reads as a clear edge without the
         harshness of the app's brighter BorderSubtle (#3C4650). -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpDivider" Color="#22344F"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpTableHeader" Color="#94A3B8"/>

    <!-- Connection status dots. Online matches SuccessBrush;
         Offline matches ErrorBrush; in-flight states get their
         own warm amber so they don't read as "everything's fine"
         or "everything's broken". -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpStatusOnline" Color="#4FA66A"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpStatusReconnect" Color="#D4A04A"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpStatusOffline" Color="#B85A4A"/>
    <!-- Room status dots. GREEN = open/joinable (waiting), BLUE = in game.
         Kept IN SYNC with the Discord webhook's embed colours (open=green,
         in_game=blue) so the same room reads the same colour in both places —
         the "traffic-light": green = go/join, blue = in progress. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpStatusWaiting" Color="#22C55E"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpStatusInGame" Color="#3B82F6"/>
    <!-- A room at capacity: warm amber dot (matches the "Full" action). -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpStatusFull" Color="#F59E0B"/>
    <!-- "Ready" checkmark green. Its own key so the InGame dot can be blue
         (matching the webhook) without turning the ✓ Listo pill blue too. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpStatusReady" Color="#22C55E"/>
    <!-- A private (password-gated) room: purple dot. The room is still
         JOINABLE (Join prompts for the password) — the dot is informational,
         it does NOT disable the action. MP-only, no external consumers. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpStatusLocked" Color="#8B5CF6"/>

    <!-- Destructive (Leave room). A touch darker than the shared
         DangerBrush so it doesn't compete with the blue primary. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpDanger" Color="#8E3F37"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpDangerHover" Color="#A65049"/>

    <!-- Ping bars (green / amber / red) — used in the rooms table
         "Ping" column and the in-room per-member RTT indicator. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpPingGood" Color="#22C55E"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpPingMedium" Color="#F59E0B"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpPingBad" Color="#EF4444"/>

    <!-- ==== Rooms-table navy refresh (mockup-driven) — MP-only, no external
         consumers, so no leak into non-MP chrome. ==== -->
    <!-- Blue-tinted panel edge for the "active rooms" + chat cards — a low-alpha
         blue (rgba(59,130,246,~0.25)) so it reads as a subtle premium rim. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpCardBorder" Color="#403B82F6"/>
    <!-- Room ROW fill — a distinct navy band so each room stands out against the
         card (MpSurface #0F1A2B), instead of blending in. Hover lifts a touch. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpRoomRowBg" Color="#16243A"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpRoomRowHover" Color="#1B2D49"/>
    <!-- MOD badge fill + outline. Deliberately DISTINCT from the row bg
         (MpRoomRowBg #16243A) so the badge stays legible on the row + hover. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpModBadgeBg" Color="#1F3358"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpModBadgeBorder" Color="#3B558A"/>
    <!-- (MpTabBackground, a navy gradient #0E1B2E → #07111F, used to live here and paint
         the multiplayer tab. It was removed rather than left declared: the design handoff
         uses ONE flat colour, MpAppBg, across the tab, the room window and the dialogs,
         and this gradient bottomed out darker than the room's background — so the two
         surfaces read as different shades of the same idea. A brush nobody paints with is
         a suggestion that it is still an option.) -->
    <!-- Subtle vertical gradient for the MOD badge (mockup "premium" look). -->
    <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="MpModBadgeGradient" StartPoint="0,0" EndPoint="0,1">
        <GradientStop Color="#2A3856" Offset="0.0"/>
        <GradientStop Color="#212D48" Offset="1.0"/>
    </LinearGradientBrush>

    <!-- =========================================================
         MULTIPLAYER REDESIGN PALETTE (design handoff 1a/1d/1e/1f —
         docs/design_handoff_multiplayer_ui/README.md, "Tokens").

         These are ADDITIVE: nothing above is retinted here, so adding
         this block changes no pixel on its own. Each redesigned screen
         migrates onto these keys in its own commit, which is what keeps
         a visual change attributable to the commit that made it.

         Why tokens and not literals in the new XAML: an audit of this
         repo found ~90 hand-written grey shorthands and three legacy
         palettes still coexisting. Pasting a fourth one inline is how
         that happened; declaring it once is how it stops.

         Alpha convention: the handoff writes rgba(...); WPF wants
         #AARRGGBB, so .09 → 0x17, .14 → 0x24, .18 → 0x2E, .3 → 0x4D,
         .55 → 0x8C. The rim colours are a light blue (#82AFFF) at low
         alpha, NOT grey — that tint is what keeps edges readable on the
         navy surfaces without a hard line.
         ========================================================= -->

    <!-- Surfaces, darkest → lightest. MpPanel is the workhorse (sub-tab bar,
         cards, right-hand panel); MpRowHighlight is a row or card that is
         yours / selected; MpField is any text input. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpAppBg" Color="#0F1C2E"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpTitleBar" Color="#233648"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpPanel" Color="#12213A"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpRowHighlight" Color="#16263E"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpField" Color="#0D1828"/>

    <!-- Inner rims. One family at four alphas — the handoff uses .09 for a
         plain card edge and .18 for an input the user is meant to aim at. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpRimFaint" Color="#1782AFFF"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpRimSoft" Color="#1C82AFFF"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpRimMedium" Color="#2482AFFF"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpRimStrong" Color="#2E82AFFF"/>
    <!-- .16 — the reference's search field, between Medium and Strong. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpRimField" Color="#2982AFFF"/>

    <!-- Text ramp, brightest → faintest. MpTextLabel is the uppercase
         section caption (9.5–10.5px, letter-spaced), deliberately the
         dimmest so it never competes with the data under it. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpTextHeading" Color="#F0F5FB"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpTextPrimary" Color="#E8EEF6"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpTextSecondary" Color="#C3D2E5"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpTextBody" Color="#B9C9DE"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpTextMuted" Color="#8EA4C0"/>
    <!-- The bottom three rungs were raised to clear 4.5:1 on MpPanel (#12213A), which they
         did not: Faint 4.09, Label 3.54, Dim 3.41 — and Label fell to 3.04 on a hovered
         room row, the worst pairing on the surface. That would be tolerable on large text
         and is not: these three are attached to the SMALLEST tokens on the surface (the
         9.5 section labels, the 10.5 pills, the 11.5 body), so the least legible colours
         were carrying the least legible sizes. Reported as text looking "washed out".

         The hue is kept and the RAMP is kept — Muted 6.30 > Faint 5.75 > Label 5.24 >
         Dim 4.55 — so the hierarchy still reads; only the floor moved up. Sizes are
         deliberately untouched: MpLabelSize stays 11.5 (see Tokens.xaml). -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpTextFaint" Color="#8C9CB1"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpTextDim" Color="#758AA5"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpTextLabel" Color="#8394B1"/>

    <!-- Semantic accents. Each has a SOLID value (dots, fills, primary
         buttons) and a TEXT value bright enough to read on the navy
         surfaces — using the solid one as text fails contrast at 11px.

         NOTE these do NOT replace MpStatusWaiting / MpStatusInGame, which
         are pinned to the Discord webhook's embed colours on purpose. Room
         status dots keep those; everything else uses these. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpAction" Color="#2F7FE0"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpActionText" Color="#8CBCF5"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpOk" Color="#4FD68A"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpOkText" Color="#8FE0B0"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpOkTextAlt" Color="#7FDCA6"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpCaution" Color="#E6B455"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpCautionText" Color="#D8BD8A"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpCautionTextAlt" Color="#F0DCAE"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpDestructiveText" Color="#D99A9A"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpDestructiveRim" Color="#4DC87878"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpPrivate" Color="#A06EF0"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpPrivateText" Color="#CBA7F2"/>

    <!-- Tinted fills + their paired rims. Always used as a pair; the rim is
         the same hue at a higher alpha so the box reads as one object. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpTabActiveBg" Color="#472F7FE0"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpTabActiveRim" Color="#6678B4FF"/>
    <!-- Idle main-nav tab text, in the title bar. Dimmer than MpTextSecondary
         because it sits ON the chrome rather than on a panel, and the active
         pill has to win against it. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpNavTabIdle" Color="#A8BCD2"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpChipOkBg" Color="#2435C46F"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpChipOkRim" Color="#4D35C46F"/>
    <!-- The same chip in the attention amber and in the failure red, at the same alpha,
         so a state chip can change what it says without changing how loud it is.
         MpCaution (#E6B455) and MpDestructiveText (#D99A9A) are the hues they tint. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpChipCautionBg" Color="#24E6B455"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpChipDangerBg" Color="#24D99A9A"/>
    <!-- Secondary text inside the OK chip (the VPN address). Deliberately a muted
         green-GREY rather than the saturated MpOkTextAlt: it sits beside the status
         word and must not compete with it. Taken from the prototype, which uses a
         value the handoff's token table doesn't list. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpChipOkDetail" Color="#A9CBB9"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpChipOkSeparator" Color="#4D78DCAA"/>
    <!-- Pill groups and the account cluster. Values read from the prototype markup,
         which carries several the README's token table doesn't list. (The rim of the
         segmented ES/EN group lived here too; that control was removed from the header
         — the language is changed in Launcher Settings — and the brush went with it
         rather than staying declared as a suggestion that it is still an option.) -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpPillActiveBg" Color="#24FFFFFF"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpPillIdleText" Color="#93A7C3"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpAccountName" Color="#E4ECF6"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpAccountElo" Color="#8FB6EA"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpChevron" Color="#8EA4C0"/>
    <!-- Unfilled capacity segment in a room row. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpCapacityEmpty" Color="#21FFFFFF"/>
    <!-- "Someone opened a room" card inside the chat flow. Fainter than the action
         tints above, so it reads as an event in the log rather than as a button. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpEventBg" Color="#172F7FE0"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpEventRim" Color="#332F7FE0"/>
    <!-- Quick-reply pill above the chat composer. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpQuickReplyBg" Color="#1F2F7FE0"/>
    <!-- The viewer's own row in the community strip's ranking. The design handoff's
         rgba(47,127,224,.12) exactly. It shares a value with MpQuickReplyBg and still gets its
         own key: they mean different things, and a shared key is how one of them ends up
         being changed for the other one's sake. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpActivityOwnRow" Color="#1F2F7FE0"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpActionSoftBg" Color="#292F7FE0"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpActionRim" Color="#8C2F7FE0"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpPrivateBg" Color="#29A06EF0"/>
    <!-- Competitive-room chip. Gold rather than a fifth hue: it is the launcher's own accent,
         and a room that puts rating on the line is exactly the row worth looking at first. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpCompetitiveBg" Color="#33C9A227"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpCompetitiveText" Color="#F0D68A"/>
    <!-- The rest of the competitive family, so the box that CREATES a competitive room
         is the same colour as the badge that marks one. It was not: the create dialog
         reused the private-room purple wholesale, so ticking "competitive" looked like
         ticking "private", and the room it produced then wore a gold badge.

         Same alphas as the private set it replaces (.08 fill, .24 rim, .30 field rim),
         over #C9A227 — the colour MpCompetitiveBg was already built from. No fifth hue
         is introduced, which is the decision the badge had already taken. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpCompetitive" Color="#C9A227"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpCompetitiveSoftBg" Color="#14C9A227"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpCompetitiveRim" Color="#3DC9A227"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpCompetitiveFieldRim" Color="#4DC9A227"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpCompetitiveTitle" Color="#F5E4B0"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpCompetitiveSub" Color="#CBB878"/>
    <!-- The tick INSIDE a filled gold box. White on #C9A227 is about 2.5:1 and reads as
         a smudge; gold is a light fill and wants dark ink, which is the same call PLAY
         already makes with #261900 on the same family. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpCompetitiveInk" Color="#261900"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpPrivateRim" Color="#3DA06EF0"/>
    <!-- The private-room BOX in the create dialog: the same hue as the chip at a
         lower alpha (.08 vs .16), because a whole box at chip strength shouts. Its
         two text tones and the password field's rim come from the prototype. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpPrivateSoftBg" Color="#14A06EF0"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpPrivateFieldRim" Color="#4DA06EF0"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpPrivateTitle" Color="#E2D5F7"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpPrivateSub" Color="#B39FD0"/>
    <!-- A ticked checklist box, and the rim of an unticked one. The green fill is the
         same hue as MpOk at .18; the amber rim is deliberately stronger (.5) because an
         EMPTY box has nothing but its outline to be seen by. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpOkBg" Color="#2E35C46F"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpCautionRimBox" Color="#80E6B455"/>
    <!-- Paired rim for MpOkBg, for a panel that is tinted green rather than a box that
         is merely ticked (the Radmin network-name field). -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpOkRim" Color="#4D35C46F"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpCautionBg" Color="#14E6B455"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpCautionRim" Color="#38E6B455"/>

    <!-- =========================================================
         CLASIFICACION / HISTORIAL / PERFIL (design handoff 3a/3b/3c).

         Everything else those three screens need already existed: MpAppBg IS the
         handoff's #0f1c2e, MpPanel IS its #12213a, MpAction its #2f7fe0, and MpOk /
         MpCaution / MpDestructiveText / MpActivityOwnRow are its greens, ambers, reds and
         own-row tint to the byte. What follows is only what was genuinely missing.

         Its TEXT colours (#61779a, #5f7592, #6d829d) are deliberately NOT adopted — they
         are the old ramp, raised since to clear 4.5:1, and copying them back would undo
         that (see .claude/rules/multiplayer.md). The existing MpText* ramp is used
         instead, and it matters more here than it did: the type sizes are back at the
         reference's values, so the faintest colours would again be carrying the smallest
         text.
         ========================================================= -->
    <!-- A match that did not count. A DIMMER panel than MpPanel, so a card the ladder
         ignored recedes behind the ones it counted without going grey. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpPanelDim" Color="#101D31"/>
    <!-- The 4px stripe down a history card, and the 3px one on the profile's recent list.
         Three states, and the third is the point: a 0.5 is not a draw, it is "nobody could
         read who won", so it gets a colour that claims nothing. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpDestructive" Color="#C8686E"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpNoResult" Color="#4A5A72"/>
    <!-- First place. The launcher's own gold would be louder than the row it sits in; this
         is the handoff's paler one, and only the number wears it. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpRankGold" Color="#F4D9A0"/>
    <!-- The hairline between table rows inside a card: fainter than MpRimFaint, which
         outlines the card itself. A separator as strong as the card's own edge makes a
         table read as a stack of cards. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpRimHair" Color="#1282AFFF"/>
    <!-- The unfilled part of the ladder's rating bar. Not MpCapacityEmpty: that one is a
         room's capacity pips and is deliberately brighter, being the thing you read. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpBarTrack" Color="#14FFFFFF"/>
    <!-- The line above the viewer's own row when it is pinned to the foot of the table —
         the same blue as the tint under it, at the strength of an edge. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpOwnRowRim" Color="#472F7FE0"/>
    <!-- PROVISIONAL, beside a name whose rating has not settled. Amber because it is a
         caveat, not a warning, and it never turns into one. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpProvisionalBg" Color="#24E6B455"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpProvisionalText" Color="#E0BE7F"/>
    <!-- The "NO CONTO" tag. Colourless on purpose: the whole claim is that this match
         says nothing, so a hue would be the wrong kind of emphasis. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpNeutralBadgeBg" Color="#12FFFFFF"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpNeutralBadgeText" Color="#A8BCD2"/>
    <!-- The amber note inside a card that explains WHY a match did not count. Fainter
         than MpCautionBg/MpCautionRim, which head a whole panel. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpNoteBg" Color="#12E6B455"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpNoteRim" Color="#2EE6B455"/>
    <!-- An inline text link ("How the ELO works", "See all history"). Reads as a link
         without being a button; it shares its value with MpAccountElo and keeps its own
         key, because those two would be changed for different reasons. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpLinkText" Color="#8FB6EA"/>
    <!-- The ghost blue button ("Rematch"): a rim at .4, softer than MpActionRim's .55,
         so a secondary action does not read as the primary one. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpActionRimSoft" Color="#662F7FE0"/>
    <!-- The profile header. The one gradient in the multiplayer surface, and it earns it:
         the header carries the single number the whole page is about. -->
    <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="MpProfileHeaderBg" StartPoint="0,0" EndPoint="1,0.35">
        <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#1B2E4C"/>
        <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#16263E"/>
    </LinearGradientBrush>

    <!-- =========================================================
         INTERACTION TOKENS — semantic colors for hover/active
         states. Centralized so changing the dorado interaction
         tint future-proofs every button at once instead of
         hunting through 23 styles for `#1FD8B66A` literals.

         Naming contract:
           Tint*  = background fill colors (low-alpha tints)
           Fg*    = foreground colors for text/icons

         Recipe per palette (paired with the role taxonomy
         documented in Styles/Buttons.xaml):

           GOLD section (mod chrome, dashboard, properties):
             SECONDARY hover = bg TintGoldHover    + fg FgHoverGold
             TERTIARY  hover = bg transparent      + fg FgHoverGold
             TERTIARY  active = bg transparent + bold + gold underline

           BLUE section (multiplayer):
             SECONDARY hover = bg TintBlueHover    + fg FgHoverBlue
             TERTIARY  hover = bg transparent      + fg FgHoverBlue

           NEUTRAL section (workshop, generic dialogs):
             SECONDARY hover = bg BgNeutralHover   + fg FgBright
             TERTIARY  hover = bg transparent      + fg FgHoverNeutral

         FgHoverGold (#FFF4E0) is a warm cream — pure white reads
         "too clinical" against the imperial dorado palette; the
         cream reads as "lit by candlelight" instead.
         ========================================================= -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TintGoldHover" Color="#1FD8B66A"/>         <!-- 12% gold -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TintGoldHoverSubtle" Color="#14D8B66A"/>   <!-- 8% gold -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TintBlueHover" Color="#1D2840"/>           <!-- = MpBlueSubtle -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TintBlueHoverSubtle" Color="#0F1A2E"/>     <!-- ~50% of above -->

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="FgBright" Color="#FFFFFF"/>                <!-- pure white, for active state -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="FgHoverGold" Color="#FFF4E0"/>             <!-- warm cream for gold palette -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="FgHoverBlue" Color="#CFE1FF"/>             <!-- soft sky for blue palette -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="FgHoverNeutral" Color="#E6EEF8"/>          <!-- cool near-white for neutral -->

    <!-- =========================================================
         PILL BADGE / DECORATIVE TINTS.
         New tokens introduced to centralize the hardcoded pill
         badge colours that used to live inline in
         ModPropertiesDialog.xaml (e.g. #33e9c176 / #55e9c176 /
         #55e63950). Anything that needs a "low-alpha accent fill"
         or "low-alpha danger outline" pulls from these tokens
         instead of hard-coding the hex itself.
         ========================================================= -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TintAccentBadge" Color="#33D8B66A"/>       <!-- 20% gold bg for pill badges -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BorderAccentBadge" Color="#55D8B66A"/>     <!-- 33% gold border for pill badges -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BorderDangerSubtle" Color="#55B85A4A"/>    <!-- 33% red border for danger-zone cards -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BgDangerSubtle" Color="#1E1614"/>          <!-- whisper red surface for danger zone cards -->


    <!-- ===================================================================
         SHARED UI RAMP (Ui*) — docs/design_handoff_ajustes_y_taller.

         That handoff makes ONE palette for the whole launcher: the settings
         windows and the Workshop drop the gold-on-grey scheme and join the blue
         one the multiplayer surface already uses, with gold reduced to a single
         job (the identity of a mod: its name in the title bar, its version pill,
         and the version figure in its General header) and nothing else.

         So the Mp* set below is no longer "the multiplayer palette" — it is the
         launcher's palette, and the handoff's token table matches it hex for hex
         (#0f1c2e = MpAppBg, #12213a = MpPanel, #2f7fe0 = MpAction, and the whole
         text / green / amber / red / purple ramps). The Mp* NAMES are kept
         because renaming ~200 brushes across the multiplayer surface would be a
         large, risky diff for no visual change; anything the handoff adds that
         is genuinely shared takes the neutral Ui* prefix instead of growing a
         misleadingly-named Mp* family.
         =================================================================== -->

    <!-- Text ramp, continuing below MpTextMuted (#8EA4C0). Counted in the
         prototype: Dim 56 uses, Ghost 37, Faint 29 — these are not accents,
         they are the working greys of a settings surface. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="UiTextDim"   Color="#6D829D"/>   <!-- de-emphasised row text, placeholders -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="UiTextFaint" Color="#61779A"/>   <!-- UPPERCASE group labels above a card -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="UiTextGhost" Color="#5F7592"/>   <!-- sidebar footer, least-important line -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="UiTextIcon"  Color="#7E93AF"/>   <!-- glyph beside a field (search, chevron) -->

    <!-- Toggle switch. The off track is white at 12%, NOT a blue tint: an off
         switch must read as "no colour", which is the whole reason a row of
         eleven of them can be scanned at a glance. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="UiToggleTrackOff" Color="#1FFFFFFF"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="UiToggleThumbOff" Color="#9FB3CD"/>

    <!-- Two more rungs of the MpRim* ladder (rgba(130,175,255,a)), both load-
         bearing in the handoff and both absent before it:
           Seam    .08 — the line BETWEEN rows inside one card. Rows are never
                         separated by margin, so this is the only thing that
                         divides them.
           Outline .20 — the 1 px ring of a ghost action button. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="UiRimSeam"    Color="#1482AFFF"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="UiRimOutline" Color="#3382AFFF"/>

    <!-- Red at 22% / 28% over the panel: the fill of a BLOCKED badge and the rim
         of a danger box. The launcher had no soft red surface before — the only
         reds were the solid MpDanger/MpDestructive and a 30% rim. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="UiDangerBadgeBg" Color="#38C87878"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="UiRimDanger"     Color="#47C87878"/>

    <!-- Two more tints of MpAction, both from the handoff: the fill of a small
         action pill ("Test" beside the Sounds switch) at 14%, and the count pill
         a sidebar entry carries when that section has something pending, at 22%. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="UiActionPillBg"      Color="#242F7FE0"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="UiActionPillHoverBg" Color="#382F7FE0"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="UiNavCountBg"        Color="#382F7FE0"/>

    <!-- The ring around the chosen half of a segmented control. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="UiSegmentActiveRim" Color="#5978B4FF"/>

    <!-- =========================================================
         TORNEOS / ESTADISTICAS (design handoff 8a-8c / 9a-9b).

         Almost nothing was needed: the two handoffs' palettes are the launcher's own,
         hex for hex, so MpAppBg / MpPanel / MpRowHighlight / MpPanelDim / MpField /
         MpAction / MpOk / MpCaution / MpDestructive and the MpText* ramp are reused.
         What follows is only what genuinely had no key.
         ========================================================= -->
    <!-- The bracket's connector lines. A LINE and not an edge, which is why it is not one
         of the MpRim* keys: those outline a surface and stop at MpRimStrong (18%), and a
         connector at that strength disappears against the page. This is the one drawing in
         the tab that carries meaning by itself - it is what says which two slots feed
         which - so it sits a step above the strongest rim. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpBracketConnector" Color="#3382AFFF"/>
    <!-- Two rungs BELOW MpTextDim, and they exist because the bracket needs three levels
         of "not the winner" where the rest of the tab needs one: the loser's name, the
         loser's marker, and a slot whose occupant is not known yet ("Ganador de X . Y").
         Dropping straight from MpTextDim to MpNoResult skips both.

         This is also how a loser is drawn. Never Opacity - house rule, and the reason is
         that opacity fades the row's background with the text and the card stops looking
         like a card. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpTextFade"  Color="#6D829D"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpTextGhost" Color="#5F7592"/>
    <!-- The empty channel of a win-rate bar for a civilization with too little behind it.
         Fainter than MpBarTrack, because that track is half of a figure somebody is
         reading and this one is the absence of one. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpBarTrackEmpty" Color="#0DFFFFFF"/>
    <!-- The losing half of a win-rate bar: red at 22% under the green. The bar is two
         colours so the balance reads without reading the number, which is the whole
         reason it is a bar and not a percentage on its own. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpBarLoss" Color="#38C87878"/>
    <!-- A bar below the leader. Dimmer than MpAction so the top row still reads as the top
         row, but a FILL and not a rim: drawn in MpActionSoftRim it was indistinguishable from
         the empty channel behind it, and a column of empty channels compares nothing. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MpBarFillDim" Color="#B82F7FE0"/>
</ResourceDictionary>
